---
import IndustryPage from "../../components/page/IndustryPage.astro";

export const prerender = false;
---

<IndustryPage
	slug="real-estate"
	eyebrow="Real estate brokerages"
	title="IT, HR and marketing for Ontario real estate brokerages"
	description="Agent onboarding at roster speed, board tools that connect to your CRM, commission administration that reconciles, and farm-area campaigns that route to the right agent."
	h1="A roster that moves faster than your systems do."
	heroLead="Independent residential, commercial, and property management brokerages. The distinguishing feature isn't the software — it's the churn. Agents join, leave, and move between brokerages at a rate that would be an emergency anywhere else, and it's Tuesday here."
	fundingText="Brokerage systems work often qualifies under Ontario's Digital Competence Centre."
	directAnswer="A brokerage's operational problem is that its people are neither quite employees nor quite outsiders. Agents need company email, CRM access, listing tools and board-connected systems on the day they sign, they take client relationships with them when they go, and the commission arrangement behind all of it has to reconcile exactly. Standard employee lifecycle tooling assumes a stability that brokerages don't have."
	differencesTitle="Three things brokerages get wrong, almost universally."
	differences={[
		{
			heading: "Onboarding speed is a recruiting argument.",
			body: "An agent who signs on Monday and is still waiting for email on Thursday has already started wondering. When a competing brokerage can have them productive the same afternoon, provisioning speed is not an IT detail — it's part of why they chose you, or didn't.",
		},
		{
			heading: "Departure is where the data leaks.",
			body: "An agent leaving takes their book with them by right. What they should not take is the brokerage's CRM export, the shared drive, or continued access to the systems they used last week. In practice most brokerages discover months later that at least one former agent still has a live login, and the conversation that follows is unpleasant.",
		},
		{
			heading: "Board tools don't integrate, and never will.",
			body: "Board-connected systems and MLS feeds were not designed to sit inside a modern stack, and no amount of wishing changes that. The realistic goal is a clean, documented boundary — one place data crosses, monitored — rather than the frictionless integration nobody can actually build.",
		},
	]}
	pillars={[
		{
			label: "Business software",
			body: "CRM chosen and configured around how your agents actually work rather than how the demo video does, connected to your listing and marketing tools. The board-tool boundary documented and watched. One number to call when an agent can't get into something on a Saturday, which is when it will happen.",
		},
		{
			label: "Back office",
			body: "Agent onboarding as a same-day process, and offboarding as a same-day process — the second one being the part that matters. Commission administration and payout records kept reconciled, and the licensing and registration documentation kept current rather than reconstructed annually.",
		},
		{
			label: "Digital presence",
			body: "Farm-area campaigns and agent-profile pages that route enquiries to the right person automatically, so a lead doesn't sit in a general inbox while three agents assume someone else has it. Recruitment campaigns too, since for most brokerages agent acquisition is the harder of the two markets.",
		},
	]}
	faq={[
		{
			q: "How quickly can a new agent be working?",
			a: "Same day, once the process is built. The build is a one-time exercise — mapping what an agent actually needs, which is usually more systems than anyone remembers — after which adding one is a form rather than a project.",
		},
		{
			q: "What happens to their access when an agent leaves?",
			a: "Revoked everywhere the same day, on a checklist that includes the systems people forget: the shared drive, the marketing tools, the listing platforms, and any group inbox they were on. We also flag what they're entitled to take, so the split is clean rather than argued about later.",
		},
		{
			q: "Can you connect our board tools to our CRM?",
			a: "Sometimes, partially, and we'll tell you honestly which. Where a proper integration isn't available we build a controlled, documented handoff instead of pretending otherwise — that's a more reliable outcome than a fragile connector that breaks every time the board updates something.",
		},
		{
			q: "Do you handle agent commission payouts?",
			a: "We handle the administration and the records — calculating against your splits, keeping statements accurate, and making sure what's paid matches what's recorded. The financial and tax treatment stays with your accountant.",
		},
	]}
	ctaTitle="Tell us what happens when an agent signs."
	ctaLead="Walk us through the last one. Everything they needed, and how long each piece took. That conversation usually finds the problem in ten minutes."
/>
